--TEST--
`Core\Request\Part::peek` answers `tainted bytes` like the part's two consumers, so a plain `bytes` binding refuses it, and its bound is a `uint` that a negative count does not meet — `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// What `peek` returns is the first octets of the same upload `readAll` and
// `content` return, read off the same connection, so it carries the same
// qualifier. A `peek` that dropped it would launder the start of every upload
// by reading it first. No runtime assertion can show that: a plain value is
// assignable into a `tainted` target, so the refused binding is the pin.
//
// The bound is a count of bytes, so a negative one is refused before the
// program runs, and the part is not read for it.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    bytes $start = $part->peek(16);
    tainted bytes $none = $part->peek(-1);
}
// covers: Core\Request\Part::peek
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
   --> case.nvs:11:20
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
